\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{A recovery site}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{x} be a vertex of a symmetric digraph carrying the
derived form of \ref{fdip-00PB}. Call #{x} a \newvocab{recovery
site} of rank #{n} when some set #{U} of vertices contains
#{x} and every neighbor #{x\pm\varepsilon_i} on the same #{n}
axes, and satisfies three conditions. The odd identification of
\ref{fdip-00PO} is available at every vertex of #{U} on those
axes, the two weights of each axis are equal units, and the
dot form #{\bar q} is nondegenerate of rank #{n} at every
vertex of #{U}. Both directions of each of those axes are
present on #{U}, so the forward and backward edges may be
identified, and the weights on the two directions agree. The
functions that are affine on #{U} in the sense of
\ref{fdip-00PK} produce odd vectors spanning the free module
of rank #{n}. The commutator square equals #{-\bar q} on the
cochains of the cells whose vertices and one-step shifts lie
in #{U}, and the vertex #{x} is one such cell.}
